    which aren't the same thing.

  I recommend the first: due date [3mis[0m the signal of caring. It's the least machinery, and the noise problem is better
  solved by tuning [3mwhen[0m alerts fire than by adding a second opt-in layer on top. We can add per-task control later if it
  actually grates — that upgrade is cheap, since it's one optional field.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Which tasks generate notifications? → Any task with a due date (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last substantive question before I lay out the design — [1mwhen alerts fire.[0m This is the noise knob, and it determines
  whether the notification store needs per-milestone dedupe state (so a task doesn't re-alert on every clock tick), so
  it shapes storage.

  - [1mOverdue only[0m — one alert per task, when it passes its due date. Minimal state, zero noise, but it only ever tells
    you you're already late.
  - [1mDue soon + overdue[0m — two milestones per task (e.g. within 24h, then past due). Actually useful as a warning, needs
    per-task-per-milestone "already fired" tracking so it doesn't repeat.
  - [1mConfigurable lead time[0m — user sets how far ahead. Flexible, but it's a settings surface and a preferences store for
    a one-user app that has no settings yet.

  I recommend due-soon + overdue with a fixed 24-hour lead. Two milestones is enough to be a warning rather than a
  postmortem, and the fixed window avoids building a settings system before we know the default is wrong.
